Suppose the point  $T$  is strictly inside the triangle, and not on the borders (the other cases will follow from this construction). Let  $\ell_a,\ell_b,\ell_c$  be the lines parallel to  $BC,CA,AB$  respectively which pass through  $T$ . Our set of segments will consist of:

(a) those segments parallel to  $BC$ , with endpoints on  $AB,AC$ , lying between  $\ell_a$  and  $BC$  (but not on  $\ell_a$ );

(b) the segments parallel to  $CA$ , with endpoints on  $AB,\ell_a$ , lying between  $\ell_b$  and  $CA$  (not on  $\ell_b$ );

(c) the segments parallel to  $AB$ , with endpoints on  $\ell_a,\ell_b$ , lying between  $\ell_c$  and  $AB$ .

Wonderful problem.  $p$  being an odd prime congruent to 2  $modulo$  3 becomes useful in the most fashionable manner. Let us dive in to the proof. (Also note that we can reduce the problem  $modulo$   $p$  as the integers are less than  $p$ )
We start by making our key claim.

<span style="color:#f00">**<u>*Claim:***  $x \not \equiv y  \pmod{p} \implies x^3 \not \equiv y^3 \pmod{p}$ , where  $x,y$  are positive integers less than  $p$ .</u></span>
<span style="color:#444">*Proof:*</span>
For the sake of contradiction assume the contrary. Let  $x$  and  $y$  be two positive integers such that  $x \not \equiv y\pmod{p}$  and  $x^3 \equiv y^3 \pmod{p}$ .  $$ x^3 \equiv y^{3}\pmod{p} $$  Now here comes the magical moment.
As  $3 \mid p-2$ ,  $\frac{p-2}{3}$  is an integer. Hence, 
\begin{align*}
(x^3)^{\frac{p-2}{3}} & \equiv (y^3)^{\frac{p-2}{3}} \pmod{p}
x^{p-2} & \equiv y^{p-2} \pmod{p} 
x^{p-1}\cdot x^{-1} & \equiv y^{p-1} \cdot y^{-1} \pmod{p} 
\implies x^{-1} & \equiv y^{-1} \pmod{p} 
\implies x & \equiv y \pmod{p}
\end{align*}
which gives us a contradiction.

Hence for every  $m$  there can at most be one  $n$  such that  $p \mid m^2 - n^3 - 1 $ . As there can be at most  $p-1$   $m$ 's there can at most be  $p-1$  members of the set satisfying the given conditions.

