\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}+\frac{1}{6\sqrt{2}}\left(\frac{a}{b}+\frac{b}{c}+\frac{c}{a}\right)+\frac{1}{6\sqrt{2}}(a+b+c)&\le\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}(a+b+c-1)
2+\frac{1}{6}\left(\frac{a}{b}+\frac{b}{c}+\frac{c}{a}\right)&\le\frac{5}{6}(a+b+c)
12+\frac{a}{b}+\frac{b}{c}+\frac{c}{a}&\le5(a+b+c)
\end{align*}
So now that we’ve moved to this simplified version we are going to dehomogenise the whole inequality; seeing as there are two degree  $0$  terms in the LHS and only one degree  $1$  term in the RHS with the condition being degree  $-1$ , we can then see it suffices to show that:
\begin{align*}
