Let \(AB < AC\) with incenter \(I\). The midpoint of arc \(BAC\) is labelled \(D\), the reflection of 
\(A\) over \(D\) is \(M\). We also define \(F\) is the intersection of the perpendicular to \(AC\) at \(C\) with \((BIC)\).
Show that \(ABFM\) is cyclic.

Now we shall use law of sines bash, let \(E\) be defined as the foot of the perpendicular 
from \(B\) to \(AD\), notice that 
\[\angle ABF = \beta + \angle CBF = \beta + (180 - (90 - \gamma) - (\angle BFC)) = 90 + \beta + \gamma - (90 - \dfrac{\alpha}{2}) = 180 - \dfrac{\alpha}{2}\]
It's obvious that \(\angle BAE = \cfrac{\alpha}{2}\). Now we obtain that \(BF \parallel AM\), thus to prove 
\(ABFM\) is cyclic, it suffices to show that \(ABFM\) is isosceles, or in other words to show that \(2AE + BF = AM = 2AD\).

We shall obtain equations for all of the terms, notice that 
    \[2AE = 2AB\cos(\dfrac{\alpha}{2})\]
We use law of sines on \(\triangle BFC\) to obtain 
\[\dfrac{BF}{\sin(90 - \gamma)} = \dfrac{BC}{\sin(90 - \dfrac{\alpha}{2})} = AB \cdot \dfrac{\sin(\alpha)}{\sin(\gamma) \cdot \cos(\dfrac{\alpha}{2})} = 2AB \cdot \dfrac{\sin(\dfrac{\alpha}{2})}{\sin(\gamma)}\]
Therefore we obtain 
    \[BF = 2AB\dfrac{\cos(\gamma)\sin(\dfrac{\alpha}{2})}{\sin(\gamma)}\]
Through law of sines on \(\triangle ABD\), we have 
\[\dfrac{AD}{\sin(\beta + \dfrac{\alpha}{2})} = \dfrac{AB}{\sin(\gamma)}\]
and thus 
\[2AD = 2AB \cdot \dfrac{\sin(\beta + \dfrac{\alpha}{2})}{\sin(\gamma)}\]
Now it suffices to show that 
\[\cos(\dfrac{\alpha}{2}) + \dfrac{\cos(\gamma)\sin(\dfrac{\alpha}{2})}{\sin(\gamma)} = \dfrac{\sin(\beta + \dfrac{\alpha}{2})}{\sin(\gamma)} \iff \sin(\gamma + \dfrac{\alpha}{2}) = \sin(\beta + \dfrac{\alpha}{2})\]
However we know that 
\[\sin(\gamma + \dfrac{\alpha}{2}) = \sin(180 - \gamma - \dfrac{\alpha}{2}) = \sin(\beta + \dfrac{\alpha}{2})\]
We are done

