The answer to both  $a)$  and  $b)$  is positive. We'll show that actually for any number of digits, there is a positive integer  $n$  such that  $P(n)$  and  $P(n^2)$  are both squares.

     $a)$  We'll prove that  $\underbrace{6\dots 6}_{2k}1^2 = \underbrace{4\dots 4}_{2k-1} 36 \underbrace{8\dots 8}_{2k-2} 921$  for all positive integers  $k$ . If  $n_{k}=\frac{2}{3}\cdot (10^{2k+1}-1)-5$ , then picking  $k = 1010$  suffices as  $P(n_{k}) = 6^{2k}$  and  $P(n_{k}^2) = 4^{2k-1}\cdot 8^{2k-2}\cdot 18^2 = (3\cdot 2^{5k-1})^2$ . To prove the above identity we simply evaluate both sides of the equation separately:
    \begin{align*}
        \underbrace{6\dots 6}_{2k}1^2 &= \left(\frac{2}{3}\cdot (10^{2k+1}-1)-5\right)^2 
                                        &= \frac{4}{9}\cdot\left(10^{2k+1}-1\right)^2-\frac{20}{3}\cdot\left(10^{2k+1}-1\right)+25
                                        &= \frac{4}{9}\cdot 10^{4k+2} - \frac{68}{9}\cdot 10^{2k+1} + \frac{289}{9}
    \end{align*}
    \begin{align*}
        \underbrace{4\dots 4}_{2k-1} 36 \underbrace{8\dots 8}_{2k-2} 921 &= \frac{4}{9}\cdot \left(10^{4k+2}-10^{2k+3}\right)+36\cdot 10^{2k+1}+\frac{8}{9}\cdot(10^{2k+1}-1)+921-888
        &= \frac{4}{9}\cdot 10^{4k+2} - \frac{68}{9}\cdot 10^{2k+1} + \frac{289}{9}
    \end{align*}
     $b)$  We'll prove that  $\underbrace{6\dots 6}_{2k+1}329^2 = \underbrace{4\dots 4}_{2k} 3994 \underbrace{2\dots 2}_{2k-2} 336241$  for all positive integers  $k$ . If  $n_{k}=\underbrace{6\dots 6}_{2k+1}329$ , then picking  $k = 1009$  suffices as  $P(n_{k}) = (3\cdot 6^{k+1})^2$  and  $P(n_{k}^2) = (3^{4}\cdot 2^{3k+2})^2$ . To prove the above identity we simply evaluate both sides of the equation separately:
    \begin{align*}
        \underbrace{6\dots 6}_{2k+1}329^2 &= \left(\frac{2}{3}\cdot (10^{2k+4}-1)-337\right)^2 
                                        &= \frac{4}{9}\cdot \left(10^{2k+4}-1\right)^2 - \frac{1348}{3}\left(10^{2k+4}-1\right) + 113569
                                        &= \frac{4}{9}\cdot 10^{4k+8} - \frac{4052}{9}10^{2k+4}+ \frac{1026169}{9}
    \end{align*}
    \begin{align*}
        \underbrace{4\dots 4}_{2k} 3994 \underbrace{2\dots 2}_{2k-2} 336241 &= \frac{4}{9}\cdot \left(10^{4k+8}-10^{2k+8}\right)+3994\cdot 10^{2k+4}+\frac{2}{9}\cdot(10^{2k+4}-1)+336241-222222
        &= \frac{4}{9}\cdot 10^{4k+8} - \frac{4052}{9}10^{2k+4}+ \frac{1026169}{9}
    \end{align*}

    The above solution proves that for all integers  $m\geq 5$ , there is a number  $n$  with  $m$  digits. Indeed,  $a)$  covers all odd numbers  $m\geq 3$ , and the construction in  $b)$  works for all even  $m\geq 6$ . The remaining small values of  $m$  can be dealt with directly: take  $n=1$ ,  $n=88$ , and  $n=1488$  respectively for  $m=1,2,4$ .

    <details><summary>Motivation & Remarks</summary>First of all, the most important thing in Yes/No problems is finding the correct answer. Here checking small cases (finding out  $88$  is the only  $\emph{nice}$  two-digit integer is kind of frustrating, but there are many three-digit ones, so we're ok) really gives you the feeling that not only should there be such  $n$ , but that there should be a lot of solutions. This is only natural as the function  $P(\bullet)$  doesn't have any particularly nice properties and we can't say much about it except that  $n$  must have an even number of  $7$ 's and  $5$ 's in order for  $P(n)$  to be a perfect square for example. Having seen [JBMO 1998 #1](https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c6h58633p358067), I knew this problem would probably rely on a similar (seemingly miraculous) identity. Trying out  $n = \overline{aa\dots aab}$ , it turns out that  $a = 6$  is actually really nice because it makes the first ~half digits of  $n^2$  fours (so we don't have to worry about them for the product). Moreover, another ~half of the decimal representation of  $n^2$  will be a continuous string of the same digit. Hence,  $P(n^2)$  will be easy to calculate this way. Plugging  $b=1$  immediately solves  $a)$ . The second part is trickier though (or at least I didn't find an easy construction). I knew the same idea had to work, so I started calculating the numbers  $\overline{666r}^2$  for  $P(r) = 6N^2$  until  $r = 329$  worked (note that this doesn't require anywhere near  $329$  checks for  $r$  due to the second condition).</details>

