Less general but quick solution. Assume contrariwise so for any sufficiently large prime  $p$  there exists no multiple of  $p$  in the sequence. Now, look at the minimum index  $a_i$  for which there exists  $j>i$  such that  $a_j \equiv a_i \pmod{p}$ . Clearly  $i \ne 1$  since then, 
\[c=a_1 \equiv a_j=a_{j-1}^3+c \pmod{p}\]
implying that  $p \mid a_{j-1}$  which is a clear contradiction. But now, 
\[0 \equiv a_j-a_i = (a_{j-1}^3 + c)-(a_{i-1}^3+c) = (a_{j-1}-a_{i-1})(a_{j-1}^2+a_{j-1}a_{i-1}+a_{i-1}^2) \pmod{p}\]
Hence, since  $a_{i-1} \not \equiv a_{j-1} \pmod{p}$  (as this contradicts minimality) we have  $p \mid a_{j-1}^2+a_{j-1}a_{i-1}+a_{i-1}^2$ . However, for any prime  $p \equiv 7 \pmod{12}$  (i.e a prime such that  $-3$  is not a QR  $\pmod{p}$ ) we have, 
\begin{align*}
a_{j-1}^2+a_{j-1}a_{i-1}+a_{i-1}^2 & \equiv 0 \pmod{p}
4a_{j-1}^2+4a_{j-1}a_{i-1}+4a_{i-1}^2 & \equiv 0 \pmod{p}
(2a_{j-1}+a_{i-1})^2 & \equiv -3a_{i-1}^2 \pmod{p}
\left(\frac{2a_{j-1}+a_{i-1}}{a_{i-1}}\right)^2 & \equiv -3 \pmod{p}
\end{align*}
which is a contradiction. Hence, it is impossible that the given sequence has only finitely many primes which proves the desired result.

