Suppose there is an  $\alpha$  such that  $f(x)\ge \alpha x$  for all integer-valued  $x$ . Take  $x=p/q$  with  $(p,q)=1$  and  $q\in\mathbb{N}$  and note that  $qf(p/q) \ge f(p)$  by iterating the identity. This would imply  $f(p/q)\ge \alpha (p/q)$ . As such, it suffices to prove the claim for integer-valued  $x$ .

Now set  $x=k\in\mathbb{N}$  and  $y=-1$  and iterate the identity above. We get  $f(k)-f(k-1)\ge -f(-1)$ . Telescoping, we find that  $f(k)-f(0)\ge -kf(-1)$ . Taking  $x=y=0$  gives  $f(0)\ge 0$ , so  $f(k)\ge -kf(-1)$ , so  $f(x)\ge \alpha x$  holds for all  $x\in\mathbb{Z}^+$  with  $\alpha=-f(-1)$ . Similarly,  $f(k)\le kf(1)$  follows by induction for any  $k\in\mathbb{N}$ , which together with  $f(k)+f(-k)\ge f(0)\ge 0$  gives  $f(-k)\ge -kf(1)$ . That is,
\begin{align*}
f(k)&\ge \alpha_1 k,\quad \alpha_1=-f(-1)
f(-k)\ge \alpha_2 (-k),\quad \alpha_2 = f(1).
\end{align*}

