It´s clear that the polyiamond lies on a triangular lattice. Color this lattice with three colors so that every equilateral triangle contains all three colors. Then it follows quickly that every isosceles triangle with sides on the lattice and with a \(120^\circ\) angle has its two base vertices of the same color.
[asy]
// triangular_grid_proper_rgb.asy
size(12cm);

real s = 1.0;
real h = sqrt(3)/2 * s;

pair A = (s,0);
pair B = (s/2,h);

int cols = 8;
int rows = 6;

pen edge = black + 0.6pt;
real r = 0.06;

// función de color global del vértice
pen vcolor(int i, int j)
{
  int t = (i + 2*j) % 3;
  if(t == 0) return red;
  if(t == 1) return green;
  return blue;
}

// dibujar vértices UNA sola vez
for(int i = 0; i <= cols; ++i){
  for(int j = 0; j <= rows; ++j){
    pair p = i*A + j*B;
    fill(circle(p, r), vcolor(i,j));
  }
}

// triángulos hacia arriba
for(int i = 0; i < cols; ++i){
  for(int j = 0; j < rows; ++j){
    pair p = i*A + j*B;
    draw(p -- p+A -- p+B -- cycle, edge);
  }
}

// triángulos hacia abajo
for(int i = 0; i < cols; ++i){
  for(int j = 0; j < rows; ++j){
    pair p = i*A + j*B;
    draw(p+A -- p+B -- p+A+B -- cycle, edge);
  }
}
[/asy]
Now place a point on each side of length \(x\) dividing it into two segments of lengths \(\lceil x/2\rceil\) and \(\lfloor x/2\rfloor\), so that the point on the side of length \(x\), the point on the side of length \(x+1\), and the common vertex form an isosceles triangle with a \(120^\circ\) angle (note that the point assigned to the segment of length \(1\) is the original vertex). This yields a chain of several points of the same color, and on the side of length \(n\) the last assigned point is at distance \(\lceil n/2\rceil\) from the initial vertex.

From this, it suffices that \(n\) be even for \(n\) to be a multiple of \(6\). The fact that \(n\) is even follows from the sum of angles being a multiple of \(120^\circ\), hence \(120\mid 180(n-2)\), which implies that \(n\) is even, thus we are done.

